  "source": {
    "title": "Armagh Guardian, 11 June 1920, p. 3 (county council count, area by area); final figures from the Frontier Sentinel, 12 June 1920, p. 5 (\"Keady Area: Sinn Fein on Top\")",
    "publisher": "Armagh Guardian (via the British Newspaper Archive)",
    "kind": "news",
    "check": "first preferences add to the printed valid poll; quota = valid / (seats + 1) + 1 as printed; each surplus equals the elected candidate's total less the quota and equals the printed transfers (plus papers with no further preference); each count is the previous count plus the transfers; every figure read from the page images. The Armagh Guardian's first-count table is misprinted (Hughes 670, M'Gahan 670, Morrison 950, Murphy 950): Hughes's 1,965 is the quota plus his printed surplus of 822 and his final figure in the Frontier Sentinel; Morrison's 985 is the Frontier Sentinel's final 987 less the 2 votes he received; Murphy's 950 fits his final 951. The four add to the printed 4,570."
  },
  "note": "Proportional representation (single transferable vote) under the Local Government (Ireland) Act 1919; polled Saturday 5 June 1920 and counted at Armagh by T. E. Reid, secretary of the county council and returning officer. Keady electoral area (three seats): the electoral divisions of Ballyards, Ballymartrim, Brootally, Crossmore, Derrynoose, Glenaul, Keady Rural, Middletown and Tynan and the urban district of Keady. 4,570 valid votes; quota 1,143. Hughes's surplus elected M'Gahan; of M'Gahan's surplus of 348 only 2 papers were transferable (he had 346 plumped votes), and Morrison was declared elected without a quota. The Armagh Guardian labels M'Gahan S.F.; the Frontier Sentinel and the Northern Whig (9 June 1920, p. 7) label him N."
}
